WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:09.580
Hello and welcome to Introduction to Blueprints for Unreal Engine 5.6.

2
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.580
In this course, we're looking to achieve a number of different things,

3
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.579
but mainly we want you to come accustomed to and have a good foundation

4
00:00:18.579 --> 00:00:26.579
in using our visual scripting language that's called Blueprints and is built into the engine.

5
00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:35.350
So what we'd like to achieve is we'd like to go over some of the basics of object-orientated programming,

6
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.350
just some nuts so that you understand what's going on.

7
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:44.670
Then we'd like you to gain an understanding of how Blueprints touch the engine

8
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.670
and where this would work.

9
00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:54.729
Next, we're going to spend a lot of time doing an exercise within the engine

10
00:00:54.729 --> 00:00:59.729
so that you can actively go in and create your own Blueprints

11
00:00:59.729 --> 00:01:05.730
using just simple node-based graphical system.

12
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:14.400
Finally, we'd like to do a little bit of work getting you to get actors to communicate with each other.

13
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.400
It doesn't make much sense yet, but it is a very useful skill

14
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:26.280
if you want to integrate some functionality into the engine.

15
00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:30.280
In this video, we're going to go over just a couple of basic concepts,

16
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:37.709
so we're all starting with a similar foundation of knowledge.

17
00:01:37.709 --> 00:01:41.870
First up, let's talk about what are Blueprints.

18
00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:47.870
Now, I think the easiest way to understand this is to go back to the roots of Unreal.

19
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.870
Unreal is a game engine at heart.

20
00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:57.870
It now works across many different industries, but it started out as a game engine.

21
00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:03.000
What's interesting about gaming, much like other industries,

22
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:08.000
is you tend to have a mix of people of different specialties.

23
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.060
Now, Unreal is written in the programming language C++.

24
00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:19.060
The thing about C++ is it's quite a complicated programming language,

25
00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:26.060
and it's a relatively big ask for everybody who works within the engine

26
00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.060
to have to go away and learn to code C++.

27
00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:34.379
So what we did here quite early on

28
00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:41.379
was we decided to expose some of the functionality of C++ into the editor.

29
00:02:41.379 --> 00:02:46.379
And for this, we created a visual scripting language,

30
00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:48.379
and that's called Blueprints.

31
00:02:48.379 --> 00:02:55.419
Now, over the years, this scripting language has grown to be very powerful.

32
00:02:55.419 --> 00:03:04.419
And at this point, there are quite a few games that are developed or released

33
00:03:04.419 --> 00:03:08.419
which are either entirely written with Blueprints

34
00:03:08.419 --> 00:03:12.419
or at least majoritatively written with Blueprints

35
00:03:12.419 --> 00:03:18.419
with just little bits of functionality got away and written in C++.

36
00:03:18.419 --> 00:03:25.340
Now, Blueprints are not necessarily as fast as C++,

37
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.340
but in most cases, they're almost comparative.

38
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:35.340
And because the code at compilation is converted to C++,

39
00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.340
it's still very fast.

40
00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:43.340
And in most cases, the code wouldn't be the bottleneck for your optimization.

41
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:51.340
So Blueprints really are a very plausible option when developing something.

42
00:03:51.340 --> 00:04:00.479
Next up, we're going to have to talk a little bit about object-orientated programming language.

43
00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:05.409
C++ is an object-orientated programming language.

44
00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:09.409
But the main thing about object-orientated

45
00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:14.409
is the code creates specific objects.

46
00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:21.410
And these objects are born of classes, which are templates for code.

47
00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:26.410
But you can have one class which can have numerous objects,

48
00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:30.410
and each of these objects can have different settings and stats.

49
00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.410
Now, this is very useful in a computer game

50
00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:39.410
where you have lots of characters and elements to be part of a game.

51
00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:45.410
All of them can share the same underlying code functionality,

52
00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:49.410
but they'll all be contained within their own objects.

53
00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:57.930
This should hopefully make more sense as we jump into the engine.

54
00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.930
Next up, something that we're going to talk about a lot

55
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.930
is the concept of classes and instances.

56
00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:10.079
So when you're in the engine, you'll see that we have a content browser,

57
00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:14.079
which is effectively Unreal's file system,

58
00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:23.079
where we can only see files which are of classes which Unreal understands.

59
00:05:23.079 --> 00:05:30.079
And these classes are templates for what ultimately would be instances.

60
00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:34.079
In this case here, we have the third person character,

61
00:05:34.079 --> 00:05:41.079
which is what you control as a pawn if you're using the third person template.

62
00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:46.079
But I've dragged three versions of it into the level, as you can see.

63
00:05:46.079 --> 00:05:51.079
So these are all three unique instances of the same class.

64
00:05:51.079 --> 00:05:55.079
And all of these would have their own settings and variables.

65
00:05:55.079 --> 00:06:00.079
For example, if they were enemies, they might all have their own health,

66
00:06:00.079 --> 00:06:05.079
which would be tracked independently on each instance.

67
00:06:05.079 --> 00:06:12.899
Now, because it's an object-orientated programming language,

68
00:06:12.899 --> 00:06:14.899
Unreal uses inheritance.

69
00:06:14.899 --> 00:06:20.899
And this is where every class that you create within Unreal

70
00:06:20.899 --> 00:06:24.899
will have a parent class associated with it

71
00:06:24.899 --> 00:06:29.899
and can have one or multiple child classes associated with it.

72
00:06:29.899 --> 00:06:36.180
Now, a child class will share the functionality and data

73
00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:40.180
of each of its parent classes.

74
00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:44.410
And therefore, this allows us to make code very modular,

75
00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:48.410
meaning that we can program a whole lot of code into one class

76
00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.410
and then just create child classes to it.

77
00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:53.410
Which would share that functionality.

78
00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:58.980
Now, this is an example of how inheritance might work.

79
00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:03.259
At the top here in pink, we have object class.

80
00:07:03.259 --> 00:07:06.259
And within C++, this would be UObject.

81
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:12.259
But this is the highest class which we will be working with in Unreal.

82
00:07:12.259 --> 00:07:19.490
And this has a lot of very low level functionality attached to it.

83
00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:25.810
The functionality that I've highlighted here is garbage collection.

84
00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.810
Now, encoding what garbage collection is,

85
00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:34.810
is when you have a class or an object or an instance

86
00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:38.810
or something in memory, and you've decided you no longer need it,

87
00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:45.290
so you mark it to be destroyed, it isn't automatically destroyed.

88
00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:51.290
So there will be some memory which would be earmarked

89
00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:54.290
for the use of that object.

90
00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:59.290
Now, it isn't automatically got rid of within C++.

91
00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:05.449
However, within Unreal, everything which inherits from the object class

92
00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:10.449
will be part of Unreal's garbage collection system.

93
00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:12.449
And that means that Unreal will go away

94
00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:18.449
and it will clean up unused instances in memory

95
00:08:18.449 --> 00:08:21.449
so that you can keep the game running smoothly.

96
00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:23.449
So it's just a whole load of functionality

97
00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:26.449
that we don't need to worry about creating ourselves

98
00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:28.449
because it's already there.

99
00:08:28.449 --> 00:08:34.779
A child class of the object, amongst many others,

100
00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:36.779
is the actor class.

101
00:08:36.779 --> 00:08:41.779
Now, the actor class is very prominent in games

102
00:08:41.779 --> 00:08:46.909
because an actor class will have a transform.

103
00:08:46.909 --> 00:08:52.909
And a transform is details of an actor's location, rotation, and scale.

104
00:08:52.909 --> 00:08:56.070
These are what's required in order to create it

105
00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.360
within a 3D space and simulation.

106
00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:03.360
Now, the actor has that functionality in it

107
00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.360
and nothing else in the class itself,

108
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:09.360
but it will be the parent class to anything

109
00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:11.360
which needs to exist within your level

110
00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:13.360
and not just in memory.

111
00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.580
The next child class of actor we have is Plaw.

112
00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:20.580
And this has the extra functionality

113
00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:24.580
of being able to be possessed and receive input

114
00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.580
from a controller.

115
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:29.929
What a controller would do is then it could possibly

116
00:09:29.929 --> 00:09:34.929
listen for keyboard, mouse, or controller inputs,

117
00:09:34.929 --> 00:09:40.059
or it could be a controller in memory,

118
00:09:40.059 --> 00:09:44.059
a stuff like an A.I. controller for the enemies, for example.

119
00:09:44.059 --> 00:09:47.279
But either way, the Plaw has the functionality

120
00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:50.279
of being possessed and receiving input.

121
00:09:50.279 --> 00:09:54.240
Now, this makes the Plaw the perfect parent class

122
00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:56.240
for the character.

123
00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.240
Now, what a character class does

124
00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.240
is it adds a couple of different components

125
00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.240
which are used for different functionality

126
00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.240
as well as some other functionality.

127
00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.460
But the character class would have a skeletal mesh, for example.

128
00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:17.460
And that's what would represent usually our character

129
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:20.460
where we would see animated within a scene.

130
00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.779
It also has something called the movement component.

131
00:10:23.779 --> 00:10:26.779
And this adds functionality and logic

132
00:10:26.779 --> 00:10:28.779
for doing stuff like running and jumping.

133
00:10:28.779 --> 00:10:34.840
So our character class is inheriting functionality

134
00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:37.840
from the Plaw to be able to be possessed,

135
00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:41.840
from the Actor to be able to exist in the world,

136
00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:45.840
and from the Object class to do a lot of low-level functionality

137
00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.840
like garbage collection.

138
00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:54.309
Classes don't need to have just one child.

139
00:10:54.309 --> 00:10:57.309
The class can have multiple children,

140
00:10:57.309 --> 00:11:00.309
and they can do multiple different functionality.

141
00:11:00.309 --> 00:11:04.309
For example, the Object doesn't have just the Actor class.

142
00:11:04.309 --> 00:11:06.309
It would also have the Actor component

143
00:11:06.309 --> 00:11:08.309
as one of its children class.

144
00:11:08.309 --> 00:11:11.309
It has the ability to be attached to an Actor.

145
00:11:11.309 --> 00:11:15.340
And there would also be a lot of data-only classes.

146
00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:18.340
So a lot of classes which are loaded into memory,

147
00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.340
but not necessarily have any visual component

148
00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.340
or exist within the world.

149
00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:28.820
That brings us to the end of our introduction and concepts.

150
00:11:28.820 --> 00:11:31.820
We will go over a number of these concepts again

151
00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.820
whilst we are in the engine and in a project.

152
00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:39.820
But that should hopefully give us a good foundation to start from.

153
00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:40.950
Thanks for listening.
